"""Unit 4.1 original textbook narrative and individually composed SVG lessons."""
from atlas_figures import shell
LESSONS=[{"title":"One program, two processes, three threads","text":"An OCR program is a set of instructions on disk. When it is started twice, the operating system creates two processes. Each process has its own address space. Threads inside one process execute separate paths but can refer to shared objects."},{"title":"What memory is shared?","text":"Picture two OCR threads in one process. Each thread keeps its active call frames separately. Both can reach the same document object on the heap. The drawing represents a teaching model: exact stack and object placement depends on the language and runtime."},{"title":"A single core schedules several threads","text":"The CPU runs one thread at a time on this one-core example. A runs, then waits for storage. The scheduler chooses B. When storage completes, A becomes runnable again. A context switch changes which thread owns the core; it does not create a second core."},{"title":"Computing time versus waiting time","text":"Hashing scanned pages uses CPU cycles. Uploading the resulting file often spends time waiting for external storage. The lengths shown are illustrative; they are not benchmarks. The useful question is which resource is limiting overall throughput."},{"title":"Synchronous and asynchronous execution","text":"In a synchronous call, the caller waits for the operation to return at that point. In asynchronous I/O, the operation can be started, another ready task can run, and the result can be awaited or handled later. Asynchrony does not itself imply parallel CPU execution."}]
